1
00:00:00,255 --> 00:00:04,293
An AI pilot has finished and the
review queue is moving faster.

2
00:00:04,764 --> 00:00:07,118
The manager asks, can we expand?

3
00:00:08,609 --> 00:00:11,266
Open the agreement the team made
before the pilot.

4
00:00:12,097 --> 00:00:15,907
This fictional scorecard covers forty
eligible pull requests before the

5
00:00:15,907 --> 00:00:17,428
pilot and forty during it.

6
00:00:17,929 --> 00:00:21,766
Count each request once, even when
several reviewers worked on it.

7
00:00:22,397 --> 00:00:25,457
Both groups cover the same service,
eligibility rule,

8
00:00:25,628 --> 00:00:26,982
and follow-up after merge.

9
00:00:27,523 --> 00:00:31,143
Expansion requires the time benefit
and every guardrail to clear.

10
00:00:31,514 --> 00:00:32,888
Keep that agreement in view.

11
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:37,967
Open one request. Start the clock when
the author marks it ready.

12
00:00:38,478 --> 00:00:41,976
Stop at the first human comment that
examines the change.

13
00:00:42,718 --> 00:00:46,056
A bot check or a quick acknowledgment
leaves the clock running.

14
00:00:46,627 --> 00:00:48,722
Use working hours on the team
calendar.

15
00:00:49,634 --> 00:00:51,791
Sort forty waits from shortest to
longest.

16
00:00:52,243 --> 00:00:54,562
Average the twentieth and twenty-first
values.

17
00:00:55,123 --> 00:00:57,966
Repeat for the other group. The
baseline median is

18
00:00:57,997 --> 00:00:59,292
eighteen business hours.

19
00:00:59,814 --> 00:01:01,434
The pilot median is thirteen.

20
00:01:02,146 --> 00:01:04,121
Five fewer business hours at the
median.

21
00:01:04,493 --> 00:01:06,829
Thirteen clears the fourteen-hour
target.

22
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:09,671
How much time did a reviewer spend
reading?

23
00:01:10,903 --> 00:01:12,571
For that, we need another measure.

24
00:01:13,941 --> 00:01:17,487
Switch to active review. These are the
minutes spent reading and

25
00:01:17,487 --> 00:01:18,806
evaluating a request.

26
00:01:19,368 --> 00:01:22,216
The hours spent waiting belong to the
first measure.

27
00:01:22,968 --> 00:01:26,427
The median was twenty-four minutes
before and twenty-six

28
00:01:26,498 --> 00:01:27,430
during the pilot.

29
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:32,399
Effort rose two minutes. The pilot is
one minute inside the agreed ceiling

30
00:01:32,399 --> 00:01:33,345
of twenty-seven.

31
00:01:34,235 --> 00:01:38,525
Carry both observations into the
meeting. The queue was shorter in this

32
00:01:38,525 --> 00:01:41,954
sample, and the typical review took a
little more work.

33
00:01:42,615 --> 00:01:46,317
Staffing or the mix of changes may
also have differed between

34
00:01:46,317 --> 00:01:47,166
the two periods.

35
00:01:48,525 --> 00:01:52,498
Count a request once when a reviewer
asks for a material change to

36
00:01:52,498 --> 00:01:54,527
behavior, tests, or scope.

37
00:01:55,107 --> 00:01:58,181
Three comments on that request still
count as one case.

38
00:01:58,652 --> 00:02:01,946
A spelling correction alone falls
outside this definition.

39
00:02:02,717 --> 00:02:05,253
Six of the forty baseline requests
needed rework.

40
00:02:05,624 --> 00:02:06,817
That is fifteen percent.

41
00:02:07,318 --> 00:02:09,974
In the pilot, seven of forty needed
rework.

42
00:02:10,405 --> 00:02:13,001
Give yourself a moment to divide seven
by forty.

43
00:02:15,934 --> 00:02:17,440
Seventeen and a half percent.

44
00:02:18,003 --> 00:02:22,194
One additional request moved the rate
two and a half percentage points above

45
00:02:22,194 --> 00:02:23,875
the fifteen-percent ceiling.

46
00:02:24,357 --> 00:02:26,119
Keep the counts beside the rates.

47
00:02:27,112 --> 00:02:31,109
Each group saw one post-merge defect
in the same follow-up window.

48
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:35,750
Record one out of forty in each. With
two events altogether,

49
00:02:36,302 --> 00:02:39,439
we have little basis for a claim about
a defect trend.

50
00:02:40,836 --> 00:02:43,774
Open the ledger. One hundred dollars
for licenses,

51
00:02:44,186 --> 00:02:48,228
twenty for the approved workspace, and
one hundred and twenty for training.

52
00:02:48,870 --> 00:02:52,861
Add the three entries. The total is
two hundred and forty dollars.

53
00:02:53,778 --> 00:02:57,121
The agreed cap is three hundred
dollars. That leaves sixty.

54
00:02:57,783 --> 00:03:01,422
Divide the total by forty eligible
requests and you get six

55
00:03:01,452 --> 00:03:02,644
dollars per request.

56
00:03:03,306 --> 00:03:05,906
Reviewer labor is still measured in
minutes here.

57
00:03:06,278 --> 00:03:08,938
Pricing that labor would require an
agreed rate.

58
00:03:10,047 --> 00:03:13,163
Time and spending clear their
thresholds. Rework misses,

59
00:03:13,514 --> 00:03:16,971
and the pilot recorded zero serious
data or access incidents.

60
00:03:17,473 --> 00:03:18,454
What would you change?

61
00:03:19,888 --> 00:03:22,812
Start with the seven requests behind
the missed guardrail.

62
00:03:23,764 --> 00:03:26,524
Read the review reason beside the
changed files.

63
00:03:26,916 --> 00:03:28,934
The ticket asks for a validator fix.

64
00:03:29,701 --> 00:03:32,875
The diff also changes an unrelated
configuration file.

65
00:03:33,266 --> 00:03:35,442
The reviewer asks the author to remove
it.

66
00:03:35,983 --> 00:03:38,317
Four of the seven cases show that
pattern.

67
00:03:39,068 --> 00:03:43,697
Recommend one adjustment. Ask authors
to name their intended files and check

68
00:03:43,697 --> 00:03:45,537
the final diff against that scope.

69
00:03:46,199 --> 00:03:49,095
Retest in the same service with the
same thresholds.

70
00:03:49,566 --> 00:03:51,721
The pattern suggests a change to try.

71
00:03:52,262 --> 00:03:55,773
It cannot tell us what caused the
difference between periods.

72
00:03:57,110 --> 00:04:00,485
Try a fresh hypothetical cohort. Fifty
eligible requests.

73
00:04:00,876 --> 00:04:02,228
Eight needed material rework.

74
00:04:02,949 --> 00:04:07,301
The ceiling is fifteen percent. Assume
every other guardrail and the time

75
00:04:07,301 --> 00:04:08,107
target are met.

76
00:04:08,978 --> 00:04:11,187
Pause here. What is the rework rate?

77
00:04:12,378 --> 00:04:16,875
What is the largest whole number of
reworked requests that would still

78
00:04:16,875 --> 00:04:17,783
meet the ceiling?

79
00:04:19,174 --> 00:04:22,936
Write both answers, then name one
piece of evidence you would want

80
00:04:23,168 --> 00:04:24,642
before recommending a change.

81
00:04:31,235 --> 00:04:34,794
Eight out of fifty is sixteen percent.
Seven out of fifty

82
00:04:34,844 --> 00:04:36,037
is fourteen percent.

83
00:04:36,678 --> 00:04:39,535
Seven is the largest whole count that
meets the ceiling.

84
00:04:40,467 --> 00:04:42,911
Would you prescribe the same fix
again?

85
00:04:44,123 --> 00:04:48,132
We have only a count. Read the review
requests and their diffs.

86
00:04:48,654 --> 00:04:52,403
If they reveal a correctable pattern,
propose one change and retest.

87
00:04:52,945 --> 00:04:54,463
If the cause remains unclear,

88
00:04:54,594 --> 00:04:56,877
investigate before choosing the
intervention.

89
00:04:57,548 --> 00:05:01,706
A defensible update can be short.
Eight of fifty requests needed rework,

90
00:05:02,037 --> 00:05:03,189
above our agreed limit.

91
00:05:03,590 --> 00:05:07,882
We will hold expansion and examine
those eight cases before choosing the

92
00:05:07,882 --> 00:05:08,459
next step.
